---
id: start-vs-nextjs
title: TanStack Start vs Next.js
description: Compare TanStack Start and Next.js for routing, rendering, server functions, caching, deployment, and the work each framework handles for you.
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Start is a good fit when you want TanStack Router's typed navigation, search parameters, and loaders in a full-stack application. Next.js is a good fit when you want the App Router's Server Component model and integrated image, font, and metadata tools. Both can build interactive applications and server-rendered websites."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This comparison was checked on September 11, 2026 against the linked documentation, including Next.js 16.3. For a broader feature matrix, see "},{"type":"link","href":"./comparison","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Start, Next.js, and React Router"}]},{"type":"text","value":". For a practical migration, see "},{"type":"link","href":"./migrate-from-next-js","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Migrate from Next.js"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"rendering-and-interactivity","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Rendering and interactivity"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Start's React components are server-rendered and hydrated by default. You can change rendering behavior per route with "},{"type":"link","href":"./guide/selective-ssr","children":[{"type":"text","value":"selective SSR"}]},{"type":"text","value":", including data-only and client-only rendering. You do not need React Server Components to use Start's server functions or SSR."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Next.js App Router layouts and pages are Server Components by default. A "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"use client"},{"type":"text","value":" boundary enables client-side state, effects, and event handlers. It applies to the imported module tree, so you do not add the directive to every interactive component. Client Components can still contribute server-rendered HTML on the initial request. "},{"type":"link","href":"https://nextjs.org/docs/app/getting-started/server-and-client-components","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Next.js component model"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Start's "},{"type":"link","href":"./guide/server-components","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Server Components support"}]},{"type":"text","value":" is "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"experimental and opt-in"}]},{"type":"text","value":". It uses client-led composition of server-produced UI. That is a different integration model from Next.js, and experimental support should not be presented as production feature parity."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"routing","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Routing"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Start inherits "},{"type":"link","href":"/router/latest/docs/guide/type-safety","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Router's routing model"}]},{"type":"text","value":". Route definitions connect the types for links, path parameters, validated search parameters, loaders, and route context. This is useful when application state lives in the URL or when many screens share typed data dependencies."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Next.js also provides compile-time checking for links through its stable, opt-in "},{"type":"link","href":"https://nextjs.org/docs/app/api-reference/config/next-config-js/typedRoutes","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"typedRoutes"}]},{"type":"text","value":" option. Describing it as only IDE hints is inaccurate. The distinction is the scope of inference and validation across your application, not whether Next.js supports TypeScript."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"In either framework, a valid route type does not prove that a database record exists or that a user may access it. Handle missing data and authorization at runtime."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For detailed navigation features, see the "},{"type":"link","href":"/router/latest/docs/comparison","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Router comparison"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"server-functions-vs-server-actions","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Server Functions vs Server Actions"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Start's "},{"type":"link","href":"./guide/server-functions","children":[{"type":"text","value":"server functions"}]},{"type":"text","value":" provide callable server endpoints with inferred inputs and outputs. You can choose GET or POST, supply an input validator, and compose "},{"type":"link","href":"./guide/middleware","children":[{"type":"text","value":"function middleware"}]},{"type":"text","value":". Use runtime validation for untrusted inputs and server-side authorization for private data."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Next.js Server Actions integrate with forms and React's pending and error-state APIs. They can validate input with a schema too. Receiving "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"FormData"},{"type":"text","value":" does not prevent validation, and a TypeScript annotation does not perform validation in either framework. "},{"type":"link","href":"https://nextjs.org/docs/app/guides/forms","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Next.js forms and validation"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Choose the API that fits your application's data flow. A typed RPC call and a form action solve overlapping problems, but they are not interchangeable in every workflow."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"caching","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Caching"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Start uses "},{"type":"link","href":"/router/latest/docs/guide/data-loading","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Router's loader cache"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for navigation data, with options such as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"staleTime"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"gcTime"},{"type":"text","value":". You can also use the "},{"type":"link","href":"/router/latest/docs/integrations/query","children":[{"type":"text","value":"official Query integration"}]},{"type":"text","value":" when you need a separate query cache. Browser data caching, server data caching, and CDN response caching serve different purposes."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Next.js documents explicit caching through Cache Components, enabled with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cacheComponents: true"},{"type":"text","value":". Its "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"use cache"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cacheLife"},{"type":"text","value":", and tag-based revalidation APIs control reuse and invalidation. Applications without that option use a different documented caching model. Compare the configuration you intend to run, rather than treating every Next.js version and configuration as having the same defaults. "},{"type":"link","href":"https://nextjs.org/docs/app/getting-started/caching","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Next.js caching"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Start's "},{"type":"link","href":"./guide/isr","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ISR guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":" describes HTTP caching and provider-specific options. A "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Cache-Control"},{"type":"text","value":" header alone does not guarantee the same regeneration, invalidation, or shared-cache behavior on every host. Verify those behaviors on your deployment target."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"build-tools","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Build tools"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Start supports "},{"type":"link","href":"./build-from-scratch","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Vite and Rsbuild"}]},{"type":"text","value":". Next.js integrates "},{"type":"link","href":"https://nextjs.org/docs/app/api-reference/turbopack","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Turbopack"}]},{"type":"text","value":", with its own supported configuration and plugin surface. Your existing build tooling can make one choice easier to adopt."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"There is no benchmark in this comparison establishing a startup, HMR, build-time, memory, or runtime-size winner. A router package's bundle size is not the bundle size of a complete Start application. Measure the same application, dependencies, rendering behavior, hardware, and production deployment before drawing performance conclusions."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"deployment","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Deployment"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Start has "},{"type":"link","href":"./guide/hosting","children":[{"type":"text","value":"deployment guides"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for several providers and runtimes. Check the guide for your chosen bundler and target, especially when you need streaming, image processing, background work, or cache invalidation."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Next.js supports self-hosting. Its image optimizer works with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"next start"},{"type":"text","value":", and its documentation covers ISR, shared caches, streaming, and multi-instance deployments. These features are not inherently Vercel-only. Operating them yourself still requires infrastructure configuration. "},{"type":"link","href":"https://nextjs.org/docs/app/guides/self-hosting","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Next.js self-hosting"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"where-next-js-has-the-advantage","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Where Next.js Has the Advantage"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Next.js is worth choosing when your application depends on its established Server Component integration, or when you want the built-in "},{"type":"link","href":"https://nextjs.org/docs/app/api-reference/components/image","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"next/image"}]},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"link","href":"https://nextjs.org/docs/app/api-reference/components/font","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"next/font"}]},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"link","href":"https://nextjs.org/docs/app/getting-started/metadata-and-og-images","children":[{"type":"text","value":"metadata conventions"}]},{"type":"text","value":". Replacing those facilities is migration work, even when alternatives exist."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If your team already maintains a working Next.js application, keep that experience and migration cost in the decision. A different routing model is not, by itself, a reason to rewrite a healthy application."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"when-to-choose-tanstack-start","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When to Choose TanStack Start"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Start is worth choosing when typed URL state, route context, explicit loaders, and callable server functions fit how you build applications. Its "},{"type":"link","href":"./guide/routing","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Router integration"}]},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"link","href":"./guide/selective-ssr","children":[{"type":"text","value":"selective SSR"}]},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"link","href":"./guide/middleware","children":[{"type":"text","value":"middleware"}]},{"type":"text","value":" are concrete reasons to try it."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Build one representative route before committing to a migration: load real data, validate search parameters, submit a mutation, handle unauthorized access, and deploy it. If those tasks become easier to maintain, you have evidence that Start fits your application. Continue with "},{"type":"link","href":"./getting-started","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Getting Started"}]},{"type":"text","value":" or the "},{"type":"link","href":"./migrate-from-next-js","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Next.js migration guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"rendering-and-interactivity","text":"Rendering and interactivity","level":2},{"id":"routing","text":"Routing","level":2},{"id":"server-functions-vs-server-actions","text":"Server Functions vs Server Actions","level":2},{"id":"caching","text":"Caching","level":2},{"id":"build-tools","text":"Build tools","level":2},{"id":"deployment","text":"Deployment","level":2},{"id":"where-next-js-has-the-advantage","text":"Where Next.js Has the Advantage","level":2},{"id":"when-to-choose-tanstack-start","text":"When to Choose TanStack Start","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nTanStack Start is a good fit when you want TanStack Router's typed navigation, search parameters, and loaders in a full-stack application. Next.js is a good fit when you want the App Router's Server Component model and integrated image, font, and metadata tools. Both can build interactive applications and server-rendered websites.\n\nThis comparison was checked on September 11, 2026 against the linked documentation, including Next.js 16.3. For a broader feature matrix, see [Start, Next.js, and React Router](./comparison). For a practical migration, see [Migrate from Next.js](./migrate-from-next-js).\n\n## Rendering and interactivity\n\nStart's React components are server-rendered and hydrated by default. You can change rendering behavior per route with [selective SSR](./guide/selective-ssr), including data-only and client-only rendering. You do not need React Server Components to use Start's server functions or SSR.\n\nNext.js App Router layouts and pages are Server Components by default. A `use client` boundary enables client-side state, effects, and event handlers. It applies to the imported module tree, so you do not add the directive to every interactive component. Client Components can still contribute server-rendered HTML on the initial request. [Next.js component model](https://nextjs.org/docs/app/getting-started/server-and-client-components).\n\nStart's [Server Components support](./guide/server-components) is **experimental and opt-in**. It uses client-led composition of server-produced UI. That is a different integration model from Next.js, and experimental support should not be presented as production feature parity.\n\n## Routing\n\nStart inherits [TanStack Router's routing model](/router/latest/docs/guide/type-safety). Route definitions connect the types for links, path parameters, validated search parameters, loaders, and route context. This is useful when application state lives in the URL or when many screens share typed data dependencies.\n\nNext.js also provides compile-time checking for links through its stable, opt-in [`typedRoutes`](https://nextjs.org/docs/app/api-reference/config/next-config-js/typedRoutes) option. Describing it as only IDE hints is inaccurate. The distinction is the scope of inference and validation across your application, not whether Next.js supports TypeScript.\n\nIn either framework, a valid route type does not prove that a database record exists or that a user may access it. Handle missing data and authorization at runtime.\n\nFor detailed navigation features, see the [Router comparison](/router/latest/docs/comparison).\n\n## Server Functions vs Server Actions\n\nStart's [server functions](./guide/server-functions) provide callable server endpoints with inferred inputs and outputs. You can choose GET or POST, supply an input validator, and compose [function middleware](./guide/middleware). Use runtime validation for untrusted inputs and server-side authorization for private data.\n\nNext.js Server Actions integrate with forms and React's pending and error-state APIs. They can validate input with a schema too. Receiving `FormData` does not prevent validation, and a TypeScript annotation does not perform validation in either framework. [Next.js forms and validation](https://nextjs.org/docs/app/guides/forms).\n\nChoose the API that fits your application's data flow. A typed RPC call and a form action solve overlapping problems, but they are not interchangeable in every workflow.\n\n## Caching\n\nStart uses [TanStack Router's loader cache](/router/latest/docs/guide/data-loading) for navigation data, with options such as `staleTime` and `gcTime`. You can also use the [official Query integration](/router/latest/docs/integrations/query) when you need a separate query cache. Browser data caching, server data caching, and CDN response caching serve different purposes.\n\nNext.js documents explicit caching through Cache Components, enabled with `cacheComponents: true`. Its `use cache`, `cacheLife`, and tag-based revalidation APIs control reuse and invalidation. Applications without that option use a different documented caching model. Compare the configuration you intend to run, rather than treating every Next.js version and configuration as having the same defaults. [Next.js caching](https://nextjs.org/docs/app/getting-started/caching).\n\nStart's [ISR guide](./guide/isr) describes HTTP caching and provider-specific options. A `Cache-Control` header alone does not guarantee the same regeneration, invalidation, or shared-cache behavior on every host. Verify those behaviors on your deployment target.\n\n## Build tools\n\nStart supports [Vite and Rsbuild](./build-from-scratch). Next.js integrates [Turbopack](https://nextjs.org/docs/app/api-reference/turbopack), with its own supported configuration and plugin surface. Your existing build tooling can make one choice easier to adopt.\n\nThere is no benchmark in this comparison establishing a startup, HMR, build-time, memory, or runtime-size winner. A router package's bundle size is not the bundle size of a complete Start application. Measure the same application, dependencies, rendering behavior, hardware, and production deployment before drawing performance conclusions.\n\n## Deployment\n\nStart has [deployment guides](./guide/hosting) for several providers and runtimes. Check the guide for your chosen bundler and target, especially when you need streaming, image processing, background work, or cache invalidation.\n\nNext.js supports self-hosting. Its image optimizer works with `next start`, and its documentation covers ISR, shared caches, streaming, and multi-instance deployments. These features are not inherently Vercel-only. Operating them yourself still requires infrastructure configuration. [Next.js self-hosting](https://nextjs.org/docs/app/guides/self-hosting).\n\n## Where Next.js Has the Advantage\n\nNext.js is worth choosing when your application depends on its established Server Component integration, or when you want the built-in [`next/image`](https://nextjs.org/docs/app/api-reference/components/image), [`next/font`](https://nextjs.org/docs/app/api-reference/components/font), and [metadata conventions](https://nextjs.org/docs/app/getting-started/metadata-and-og-images). Replacing those facilities is migration work, even when alternatives exist.\n\nIf your team already maintains a working Next.js application, keep that experience and migration cost in the decision. A different routing model is not, by itself, a reason to rewrite a healthy application.\n\n## When to Choose TanStack Start\n\nStart is worth choosing when typed URL state, route context, explicit loaders, and callable server functions fit how you build applications. Its [Router integration](./guide/routing), [selective SSR](./guide/selective-ssr), and [middleware](./guide/middleware) are concrete reasons to try it.\n\nBuild one representative route before committing to a migration: load real data, validate search parameters, submit a mutation, handle unauthorized access, and deploy it. If those tasks become easier to maintain, you have evidence that Start fits your application. Continue with [Getting Started](./getting-started) or the [Next.js migration guide](./migrate-from-next-js).\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\nid: start-vs-nextjs\ntitle: TanStack Start vs Next.js\ndescription: Compare TanStack Start and Next.js for routing, rendering, server functions, caching, deployment, and the work each framework handles for you.\n---\n\nTanStack Start is a good fit when you want TanStack Router's typed navigation, search parameters, and loaders in a full-stack application. Next.js is a good fit when you want the App Router's Server Component model and integrated image, font, and metadata tools. Both can build interactive applications and server-rendered websites.\n\nThis comparison was checked on September 11, 2026 against the linked documentation, including Next.js 16.3. For a broader feature matrix, see [Start, Next.js, and React Router](./comparison). For a practical migration, see [Migrate from Next.js](./migrate-from-next-js).\n\n## Rendering and interactivity\n\nStart's React components are server-rendered and hydrated by default. You can change rendering behavior per route with [selective SSR](./guide/selective-ssr), including data-only and client-only rendering. You do not need React Server Components to use Start's server functions or SSR.\n\nNext.js App Router layouts and pages are Server Components by default. A `use client` boundary enables client-side state, effects, and event handlers. It applies to the imported module tree, so you do not add the directive to every interactive component. Client Components can still contribute server-rendered HTML on the initial request. [Next.js component model](https://nextjs.org/docs/app/getting-started/server-and-client-components).\n\nStart's [Server Components support](./guide/server-components) is **experimental and opt-in**. It uses client-led composition of server-produced UI. That is a different integration model from Next.js, and experimental support should not be presented as production feature parity.\n\n## Routing\n\nStart inherits [TanStack Router's routing model](/router/latest/docs/guide/type-safety). Route definitions connect the types for links, path parameters, validated search parameters, loaders, and route context. This is useful when application state lives in the URL or when many screens share typed data dependencies.\n\nNext.js also provides compile-time checking for links through its stable, opt-in [`typedRoutes`](https://nextjs.org/docs/app/api-reference/config/next-config-js/typedRoutes) option. Describing it as only IDE hints is inaccurate. The distinction is the scope of inference and validation across your application, not whether Next.js supports TypeScript.\n\nIn either framework, a valid route type does not prove that a database record exists or that a user may access it. Handle missing data and authorization at runtime.\n\nFor detailed navigation features, see the [Router comparison](/router/latest/docs/comparison).\n\n## Server Functions vs Server Actions\n\nStart's [server functions](./guide/server-functions) provide callable server endpoints with inferred inputs and outputs. You can choose GET or POST, supply an input validator, and compose [function middleware](./guide/middleware). Use runtime validation for untrusted inputs and server-side authorization for private data.\n\nNext.js Server Actions integrate with forms and React's pending and error-state APIs. They can validate input with a schema too. Receiving `FormData` does not prevent validation, and a TypeScript annotation does not perform validation in either framework. [Next.js forms and validation](https://nextjs.org/docs/app/guides/forms).\n\nChoose the API that fits your application's data flow. A typed RPC call and a form action solve overlapping problems, but they are not interchangeable in every workflow.\n\n## Caching\n\nStart uses [TanStack Router's loader cache](/router/latest/docs/guide/data-loading) for navigation data, with options such as `staleTime` and `gcTime`. You can also use the [official Query integration](/router/latest/docs/integrations/query) when you need a separate query cache. Browser data caching, server data caching, and CDN response caching serve different purposes.\n\nNext.js documents explicit caching through Cache Components, enabled with `cacheComponents: true`. Its `use cache`, `cacheLife`, and tag-based revalidation APIs control reuse and invalidation. Applications without that option use a different documented caching model. Compare the configuration you intend to run, rather than treating every Next.js version and configuration as having the same defaults. [Next.js caching](https://nextjs.org/docs/app/getting-started/caching).\n\nStart's [ISR guide](./guide/isr) describes HTTP caching and provider-specific options. A `Cache-Control` header alone does not guarantee the same regeneration, invalidation, or shared-cache behavior on every host. Verify those behaviors on your deployment target.\n\n## Build tools\n\nStart supports [Vite and Rsbuild](./build-from-scratch). Next.js integrates [Turbopack](https://nextjs.org/docs/app/api-reference/turbopack), with its own supported configuration and plugin surface. Your existing build tooling can make one choice easier to adopt.\n\nThere is no benchmark in this comparison establishing a startup, HMR, build-time, memory, or runtime-size winner. A router package's bundle size is not the bundle size of a complete Start application. Measure the same application, dependencies, rendering behavior, hardware, and production deployment before drawing performance conclusions.\n\n## Deployment\n\nStart has [deployment guides](./guide/hosting) for several providers and runtimes. Check the guide for your chosen bundler and target, especially when you need streaming, image processing, background work, or cache invalidation.\n\nNext.js supports self-hosting. Its image optimizer works with `next start`, and its documentation covers ISR, shared caches, streaming, and multi-instance deployments. These features are not inherently Vercel-only. Operating them yourself still requires infrastructure configuration. [Next.js self-hosting](https://nextjs.org/docs/app/guides/self-hosting).\n\n## Where Next.js Has the Advantage\n\nNext.js is worth choosing when your application depends on its established Server Component integration, or when you want the built-in [`next/image`](https://nextjs.org/docs/app/api-reference/components/image), [`next/font`](https://nextjs.org/docs/app/api-reference/components/font), and [metadata conventions](https://nextjs.org/docs/app/getting-started/metadata-and-og-images). Replacing those facilities is migration work, even when alternatives exist.\n\nIf your team already maintains a working Next.js application, keep that experience and migration cost in the decision. A different routing model is not, by itself, a reason to rewrite a healthy application.\n\n## When to Choose TanStack Start\n\nStart is worth choosing when typed URL state, route context, explicit loaders, and callable server functions fit how you build applications. Its [Router integration](./guide/routing), [selective SSR](./guide/selective-ssr), and [middleware](./guide/middleware) are concrete reasons to try it.\n\nBuild one representative route before committing to a migration: load real data, validate search parameters, submit a mutation, handle unauthorized access, and deploy it. If those tasks become easier to maintain, you have evidence that Start fits your application. Continue with [Getting Started](./getting-started) or the [Next.js migration guide](./migrate-from-next-js).\n"}
